  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe change to a cooler wetter weather pattern is beginning today.\nThe flow aloft is becoming a bit more westerly today, as the upper\nlevel ridge breaks down. One disturbance is approaching the area\nfrom the north this morning, while another disturbance is\napproaching Southwest MT from the southwest. Overall expect shower\nand thunderstorm activity to start to increase late this morning\nthrough this afternoon. A few storms could produce strong wind\ngusts or heavy rainfall this afternoon/evening. The latest HREF\ndoes have one strong storm moving southeastward through North\nCentral MT this evening, with the main impact being\nlightning/strong wind gusts. Otherwise, afternoon temperatures to\nstart to cool a bit each day through Friday.\n\nThe chance for thunderstorms will increase Thu into Friday morning\nas precipitable water increases. Thus the chance for isolated\nflash flooding is possible from heavier showers. Additionally, a\nfew storms could produce strong wind gusts on Thursday/Friday, \nespecially over Southwest MT where it will be a bit more unstable.\n\nThe summertime upper level ridge of high pressure will try to\nrebuild over the northern Rockies starting this weekend. This will\nresult in warmer, but more unstable air moving back into the CWA\nthis weekend. Expect daily chances for showers/thunderstorms from\nSaturday through Tuesday. Again, the main concern from storms over\nthe weekend will be lightning and strong wind gusts.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nAfter a few days of cooler temperatures, warmer air moves back\ninto the CWA this weekend. Thus afternoon temperatures could be a\ntouch warmer than currently forecast, especially if some of the\nsmoke clears out a bit.\n\nThe main concern from thunderstorms over the next few days will be\nlightning, brief heavy rainfall and strong wind gusts. Any\nlightning that occurs away from the main storm will be capable of\nproducing new wildfires. Brusda",
